Chapter: Build Settings

 LOAD-BEARING: values used in emit functions (code-buffer-size,
 data-buffer-size, accum-capacity, offset-table-size, offset-table-mask)
 are baked into the seed as x86-64 immediates. Changing them requires
 a seed rebuild -- the old seed silently uses old values otherwise.

Section: Emit Buffers
  code-buffer-size : Integer = 8388608
  data-buffer-size : Integer = 2097152
  accum-capacity : Integer = 65536

Section: Work Budgets
  max-emit-work : Integer = 16777216
  max-parse-fuel : Integer = 16777216

 max-parse-fuel is a budget for the WHOLE unit, not a bound on recursion,
 and the difference is what a silent crash cost. A parse arm that returns
 without consuming a token spins, and each turn costs a few stack frames
 but only one fuel: the stack runs out a gigabyte down, millions of frames
 deep, long before sixteen million fuel does, so the guard cannot fire and
 the compile double-faults with no diagnostic. Lowering the budget is not
 the answer -- it is sized for the unit's real work, and a budget below
 that cascades into a heap blow-up instead, because an exhausted parse
 call returns an error node and the parse carries on.

  max-parse-stall : Integer = 4096

 The O(N) cost this bound was sized against is GONE. A list literal used
 to take one live local per element -- emit-eval-list-elems gave every
 element its own local and held all of them until the buffer was filled
 -- and a local is addressed by a number carried in EmitResult.reg, a
 field bounded to 65535, with spill slots starting at spill-base (32),
 so 65504 was the most a function could have. The emitter now reserves
 the buffer first and stores each element as it is evaluated, which
 costs TWO locals at any length (X86_64Compound, List Emission).

 What the bound was protecting against, and why the check still lives in
 the checker rather than the emitter: past the ceiling the emitter walked
 off the end of four separate 16-bit counters in sequence -- the slot
 number first, then next-local, then spill-count, then next-temp -- and
 the first one to go tripped __narrow, which traps with an invalid opcode.
 A list literal no longer reaches that chain, but the counters are still
 16 bits and a function that acquires that many locals by any other route
 still would.

 There was no diagnostic on that path and there could not be one, for a
 reason that has nothing to do with the emitter's reporting: __narrow
 traps. The failure is a hardware fault at the instruction, not a branch
 the emitter reaches and could report from. Refusing the literal at check
 time is what gets the author a source span and a sentence instead of a
 register dump.

 An earlier version of this paragraph said instead that a diagnostic
 raised inside emit-function is dropped, because codegen-carry-forward
 takes the bag from the state before the function. That is FALSE and it
 was measured false: st-add-error and its siblings are __record-set on
 the bag, __record-set mutates in place and returns the same record, and
 reset-func-state is itself a chain of __record-set, so the state
 emit-function works on is the record the caller is holding. The bag
 propagates by mutation. A bounded return type makes emit-return-guard
 raise CDX4010 from inside emit-function and it arrives with its span;
 codex/test/range-let-carry pins exactly that through a bounded field
 store. The claim is recorded here only because it survived two entries
 in the register and this file before anyone compiled a probe.

  max-list-literal-elems : Integer = 65504

 inline-cost-ceiling caps the body cost a definition may have and still
 be inlined at EVERY saturated call site by the opt-in cost-based pass
 (`inline-cost-based`, IR/Lowering.codex). Unlike the leaf and
 single-caller passes, that pass duplicates on purpose, so the ceiling
 bounds the size it can add: each site pays at most this many IR
 operation nodes. The value is measured, not derived. On the selfhost
 (2026-07-20): ceiling 4 grew the binary 1.1 per cent, 6 grew it 5.0,
 8 grew it 5.5, 12 grew it 19 -- and none of them made the compiled
 compiler faster (isolated with the pass switched off in the timed
 compile: baseline 11.3-11.5 s, ceiling-4 binary 11.6-11.9 s,
 ceiling-8 binary 12.6-14.3 s). Four is the least-bad setting, kept
 for workloads that are not the compiler; the measurements are why the
 pass is not in the default pipeline.

  inline-cost-ceiling : Integer = 4

Section: Demand Decks

 Every phase deck is a generous fixed floor. Address space is the only
 cost - the boot demand-pages the heap range and physical memory
 commits on touch - so the floors are sized for the arena, not for the
 source. These replaced the survey multiplier system, which sized decks
 from source length and corrupted silently when it under-reserved.

 A floor that is not generous ENOUGH used to fail the same way the
 multiplier did: the parse keep-deck copy (copy-sx-document) wrote past
 the floor into the scratch it is still reading, the copier dereferenced
 its own overwrite, and the compile died in a #GP with no diagnostic --
 the post-copy overflow check never ran. A 444 KB single-unit source
 (the GopBoot boot bundle) was the first to push the copied CST past
 64 MB. Since 2026-07-20 the parse keep copy SAYS SO
 instead: a guard band the width of demand-parse-guard-band sits
 between the keep reservation and the scratch, the def and type-def
 copy loops stop at the floor, and the phase raises CDX9002 -- fired
 rather than assumed, by starving this keep floor to 2 MB and watching
 the selfhost compile refuse with the diagnostic. The CHECK-KEEP deep
 copy carries the same discipline (its mc-ceiling).
 The LEX deck carries the same discipline since 2026-07-21:
 tokenize-collect reads __deck-pos every token and stops at
 demand-lex-guard-band below the reservation ceiling. It is the phase
 that starving demonstrably reaches FIRST -- `compile.ps1 -Decks 5`
 used to die in a #GP inside __linked_list_to_list, and the prose here
 said that crash was an over-full parse SCRATCH colliding with the
 bivy. Measured 2026-07-21, it was not: at 5 per cent the parse
 scratch fits (12.6 MB used against a 19.2 MB floor) and the LEX deck
 does not (28.7 MB against 4.8 MB), so the deck grew into the bivy
 holding the token linked list and the crash was the lexer reading its
 own overwrite one call later.

 DESUGAR carries the same discipline since 2026-07-21, and finding it
 needed the phase trace: `-Decks` at 30, 40, 50 and 60 all complete LEX
 and PARSE and then die inside DESUGAR, in three different shared
 runtime helpers, so the crash site named nothing. This section said
 DESUGAR had "no bulk write path to hang a check on". It has four --
 desugar-defs-ll, desugar-type-defs-ll, rewrite-constrained-defs and
 insert-dicts-at-call-sites, each one iteration per definition -- and
 they now read __deck-pos per definition and stop a
 demand-desugar-guard-band short of the ceiling. The band is sized for
 ONE definition's desugared tree plus the wind-down, because the check
 runs before a definition is desugared rather than after, and it is
 deliberately generous: a band that is too big costs a spurious CDX9002
 that says exactly what to do about it, and a band that is too small
 costs silent corruption. The asymmetry is the whole argument.

 SCOPE, CHECK and LOWER joined them on 2026-07-21, and RESOLVE and LIFT
 on the same day, so every phase that reserves a deck now stops on the
 write path rather than reporting damage afterwards. SCOPE is the one
 worth remembering: its first guard went on scope-adefs-ll, which
 writes 0.3 MB of the phase's 56.3, and the phase went on dying in a
 divide error at a starved floor because the walk that writes the other
 53.3 was unguarded. The lesson is that a guard belongs where the bytes
 are, and that where the bytes are is a measurement, not a reading of
 the code. RESOLVE and LIFT were both measured with deck-pos probes
 before either guard was written.

 RESOLVE and LIFT joined them on 2026-07-21, and finding out how cost
 more than writing them. A loop inside a phase-wide deck-record extent
 reads a FROZEN __deck-pos -- the cell is only written back when the
 nesting counter returns to zero -- so deck-short-of there compares the
 floor against the band and never mentions the usage. Both of these
 phases are that shape and both use deck-bound-short-of instead, which
 reads the live frontier. The full account is in PhaseAllocator beside
 the two predicates; the measurement is an in-loop probe that printed
 __deck-pos unchanged through 19.1 MB of writing.

 LOWER and the copy sit inside phase-wide extents and both read the
 frozen cell, so both guards were dead from the day they shipped. Both
 now read R10. The measurement that settles it is MEASURE against a
 16 MB LOWER floor: with the cell, LOWER reports `used=-5307132`,
 negative because the phase overran far enough to destroy the record
 measuring it; with R10, it stops at 8,487,976 bytes, one guard band
 short of the floor and reporting CDX9002 without a crash.

 CHECK is the exception and the reason is three lines of check-chapter:
 it issues a __deck-exit immediately before the per-definition walk and
 a __deck-enter after it, so that walk runs OUTSIDE the extent and the
 cell is live. Swapping CHECK to deck-bound-short-of reads the BIVY
 pointer, which is above every deck ceiling, and truncates the walk on
 its first definition -- that is the "truncates silently on an
 eight-kilobyte program" this paragraph used to attribute to all three.
 It was CHECK alone, it was not silent (40-odd CDX2000 emit errors), and
 the caller-side report was never the problem: `check-sat` and
 `lower-sat` are both evaluated after their extents have exited, where
 the cell is live.

 That verdict was noise until 2026-07-21, and the reason is worth
 carrying: check-deck-overflow used to read its operands out of a
 PhaseMetrics, and phase-measure builds that record with deck-record at
 the END of the phase -- so an overflowing phase allocated the record
 describing its own overflow past its own reservation and then
 overwrote it. It now takes plain Integers captured from __deck-pos.

 **LOWER's headroom is 1.06x, not the 1.11x this file used to record.**
 Measured 2026-07-21 in CDX mode: -Decks 93 refuses and 94 compiles, so
 LOWER uses 312 to 315 MB of its 335.5 MB floor. The old figure came
 from -Measure, which runs no IR pipeline and therefore measures a
 smaller LOWER than the one that ships. It is by a wide margin the
 tightest deck in the compiler and the row to watch.

 The PARSE floors were not generous, whatever the comment above claimed.
 On 2026-07-13 the compiler grew by about 80 KB of source - the foreword
 chapters behind the quotation gate, chiefly Ed25519 and Sha256 - and
 PARSE overflowed at 192 MB. The floor had been sitting within roughly a
 kilobyte of its limit: the build before the last one passed, and adding
 a single function to a prose block tipped it over. Nobody had noticed
 because nothing had grown the compiler in a while.

 That is the failure this section warns about, arriving exactly as
 predicted, and it is the reason these two are now 384 MB rather than
 192. A floor you can reach by writing one more function is not a floor.

  demand-lex-floor : Integer = 100663296
  demand-lex-guard-band : Integer = 1048576
  demand-parse-keep-floor : Integer = 402653184
  demand-parse-guard-band : Integer = 67108864
  demand-parse-scratch-floor : Integer = 411041792
  demand-parse-scratch-guard-band : Integer = 8388608
  demand-desugar-floor : Integer = 75497472
  demand-desugar-guard-band : Integer = 8388608
  demand-scope-floor : Integer = 109051904
  demand-scope-guard-band : Integer = 8388608
  demand-check-floor : Integer = 679477248
  demand-check-guard-band : Integer = 8388608
  demand-check-keep-floor : Integer = 100663296
  demand-frontend-keep-floor : Integer = 209715200
  demand-frontend-keep-guard-band : Integer = 8388608
  demand-lower-floor : Integer = 343932928
  demand-lower-guard-band : Integer = 8388608
  demand-resolve-floor : Integer = 209715200
  demand-resolve-guard-band : Integer = 8388608
  demand-lift-floor : Integer = 109051904
